{"word": "civilized", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "civilized", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈsɪv.ɪ.laɪzd/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "more civilized", "tags": ["comparative"]}, {"word": "most civilized", "tags": ["superlative"]}, {"word": "civilised", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Having a highly developed society or culture; belonging to civilization.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "In the year 1521, when Cortez reached Anahuac, the American bison was seen for the first time by civilized Europeans, if we may be permitted to thus characterize the horde of blood thirsty plunder seekers who fought their way to the Aztec capital.", "reference": "1889, William T. Hornaday, The Extermination of the American Bison:"}, {"text": "Like the wolf, man the hunter in killing the weak and unfit, actually works to maintain the health of the herd. But civilized man is quite a different animal, and when he wipes out an entire city or levels a forest, he is no longer working within the natural balance of things.", "reference": "1981, William Irwin Thompson, The Time Falling Bodies Take to Light: Mythology, Sexuality and the Origins of Culture, London: Rider/Hutchinson & Co., page 196:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "hy", "name": "Armenian"}, "word": "քաղաքակիրթ"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "цивилизован"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "културен"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "възпи́тан"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "civilitzat"}, {"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "文明的"}, {"language": {"code": "cs", "name": "Czech"}, "word": "civilizovaný"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "beschaafd"}, {"language":